nce $(ka)$ is a real number, we conclude that $\\frac{ka}{x}\\in F,$ i.e., $k\\cdot f(x)\\in F.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139537079 17592186576411 false] [17592186440654 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$g(x)\\in F,$ where $g(x)=\\frac{1}{2x}.$” is true. The set $F$ contains only functions of the form $\\frac{a}{x},$ where $a$ is a real number. The function $g(x)=\\frac{1}{2x}$ is a function of this form with $a=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, $g(x)\\in F.$\n\n- Statement “For any $f(x)\\in F$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in F.$” is true. Indeed, suppose $f(x)=\\frac{a}{x}\\in F,$ where $a\\in R,$ and $k\\in R.$ Then,\n\n$$\nk\\cdot f(x)=k\\cdot (\\frac{a}{x})=\\frac{ka}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $(ka)$ is a real number, we conclude that $\\frac{ka}{x}\\in F,$ i.e., $k\\cdot f(x)\\in F.$\n\n- Statement “The set is closed under scalar multiplication” is true. Since statement “For any $f(x)\\in F$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in F.$” is true, the set is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$g(x)\\in F,$ where $g(x)=\\frac{1}{2x}.$”; “For any $f(x)\\in F$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in F.$”; “The set is closed under scalar multiplication”." 13194139537079 17592186576411 true] [17592186440655 1071 "Let $T$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{11}{a}_{21}+{a}_{12}{a}_{22}>0.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The identity matrix ${I}_{2}$ belongs to $T.$\n\nII. For every $A\\in T$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha \\cdot A\\in T.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." 13194139537079 17592186576411 false] [17592186440655 1071 "Let $T$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{11}{a