at apply." 13194139537079 17592186576411 true] [17592186481762 1071 "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n- **I.** ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **II.** ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$\n- **III.** ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537079 17592186576411 false] [17592186481762 1074 "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[2\\sqrt{x-1}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2\\sqrt{2-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(2\\sqrt{a-1}-2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|-1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|) \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{x}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a}+\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d}x$ and ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$ are divergent." 13194139537079 17592186576411 true] [17592186481762 1074 "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\,\\text{d