ust satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement ${f}^{′}(x)=-5{x}^{2}$ is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-2{x}^{3},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-2{x}^{3})}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -5{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true. Substituting $y=-2{x}^{3}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6{x}^{2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3y}{x} \\\\ -6{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3(-2{x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ -6{x}^{2} & =-6{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” is true. Since statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true, the function $y=-2{x}^{3}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement”; “The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation” only." 13194139537079 17592186576411 true] [17592186494077 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-2{x}^{3},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-2{x}^{3})}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =-6{x}^{2} \\\\  & \\ne -5{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-2{x}^{3}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6{x}^{2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3y}{x} \\\\ -6{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3(-2{x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ -6{x}^{2} & =-6{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-2{x}^{3}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139537079 17592186576411 false] [17592186494079 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${f}^{′}(x)=-7{x}^{3}$\n- **II.** Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- **III.** The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" 13194139537079 17592186576411 false] [17592186494079 1071 "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." 13194139537079 17592186576411 true] [17592186494079 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement ${f}^{′}(x)=-7{x}^{3}$ is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{4})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-12{x}^{3} \\\\  & \\ne -7{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement” is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{4}$ and ${y}^{′}=-12{x}^{3}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ -12{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(-3{x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ -12{x}^{3} & =-12{x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “The fun